<b>To the Shareholders of Berkshire Hathaway Inc.:</b>

     Our gain in net worth during 1996 was $6.2 billion, or 36.1%.  Per-
share book value, however, grew by less, 31.8%, because the number of=20
Berkshire shares increased:  We issued stock in acquiring FlightSafety=20
International and also sold new Class B shares.*   Over the last 32 years=
=20
(that is, since present management took over) per-share book value has=20
grown from $19 to $19,011, or at a rate of 23.8% compounded annually.
<i>
 * Each Class B share has an economic interest equal to 1/30th of=20
   that possessed by a Class A share, which is the new designation for =20
   the only stock that Berkshire had outstanding before May 1996. =20
   Throughout this report, we state all per-share figures in terms of
   "Class A equivalents," which are the sum of the Class A shares=20
   outstanding and 1/30th of the Class B shares outstanding.
</i>
     For technical reasons, we have restated our 1995 financial=20
statements, a matter that requires me to present one of my less-than-
thrilling explanations of accounting arcana.  I'll make it brief.

     The restatement was required because GEICO became a wholly-owned=20
subsidiary of Berkshire on January 2, 1996, whereas it was previously=20
classified as an investment.  From an economic viewpoint - taking into=20
account major tax efficiencies and other benefits we gained - the value=20
of the 51% of GEICO we owned at year-end 1995 <i>increased</i> significantl=
y=20
when we acquired the remaining 49% of the company two days later. =20
Accounting rules applicable to this type of "step acquisition," however,=20
required us to <i>write down</i> the value of our 51% at the time we moved =
to=20
100%.  That writedown - which also, of course, reduced book value -=20
amounted to $478.4 million.  As a result, we now carry our original 51%=20
of GEICO at a value that is both lower than its market value at the time=20
we purchased the remaining 49% of the company and lower than the value at=
=20
which we carry that 49% itself.

     There is an offset, however, to the reduction in book value I have=20
just described:  Twice during 1996 we issued Berkshire shares at a=20
premium to book value, first in May when we sold the B shares for cash=20
and again in December when we used both A and B shares as part-payment=20
for FlightSafety.  In total, the three non-operational items affecting=20
book value contributed less than one percentage point to our 31.8% per-
share gain last year.

     I dwell on this rise in per-share book value because it roughly=20
indicates our economic progress during the year.  But, as Charlie Munger,=
=20
Berkshire's Vice Chairman, and I have repeatedly told you, what counts at=
=20
Berkshire is intrinsic value, not book value.  The last time you got that=
=20
message from us was in the Owner's Manual, sent to you in June after we=20
issued the Class B shares.  In that manual, we not only defined certain=20
key terms - such as intrinsic value -  but also set forth our economic=20
principles.

     For many years, we have listed these principles in the front of our=20
annual report, but in this report, on pages 58 to 67, we reproduce the=20
entire Owner's Manual.  In this letter, we will occasionally refer to the=
=20
manual so that we can avoid repeating certain definitions and=20
explanations.  For example, if you wish to brush up on "intrinsic value,"=
=20
see pages 64 and 65.

     Last year, for the first time, we supplied you with a table that=20
Charlie and I believe will help anyone trying to estimate Berkshire's=20
intrinsic value.  In the updated version of that table, which follows, we=
=20
trace two key indices of value.  The first column lists our per-share=20
ownership of investments (including cash and equivalents) and the second=20
column shows our per-share earnings from Berkshire's operating businesses=
=20
before taxes and purchase-accounting adjustments but after all interest=20
and corporate overhead expenses.  The operating-earnings column excludes
all dividends, interest and capital gains that we realized from the=20
investments presented in the first column.  In effect, the two columns=20
show what Berkshire would have reported had it been broken into two parts.


