     As the table tells you, our investments per share increased in 1996=20
by 29.0% and our non-investment earnings grew by 63.2%.  Our goal is to=20
keep the numbers in both columns moving ahead at a reasonable (or, better=
=20
yet, unreasonable) pace.

     Our expectations, however, are tempered by two realities.  First,=20
our past rates of growth cannot be matched nor even approached: =20
Berkshire's equity capital is now large - in fact, fewer than ten=20
businesses in America have capital larger -  and an abundance of funds=20
tends to dampen returns.  Second, whatever our rate of progress, it will=20
not be smooth:  Year-to-year moves in the first column of the table above=
=20
will be influenced in a major way by fluctuations in securities markets;=20
the figures in the second column will be affected by wide swings in the=20
profitability of our catastrophe-reinsurance business.

     In the table, the donations made pursuant to our shareholder-
designated contributions program are charged against the second column,=20
though we view them as a shareholder benefit rather than as an expense. =20
All other corporate expenses are also charged against the second column.=20
These costs may be lower than those of any other large American=20
corporation:  Our after-tax headquarters expense amounts to less than two=
=20
basis points (1/50th of 1%) measured against net worth.  Even so, Charlie=
=20
used to think this expense percentage outrageously high, blaming it on my=
=20
use of Berkshire's corporate jet,<i> The Indefensible</i>.  But Charlie has=
=20
recently experienced a "counter-revelation":  With our purchase of=20
FlightSafety, whose major activity is the training of corporate pilots,=20
he now rhapsodizes at the mere mention of jets.

     Seriously, costs matter.  For example, equity mutual funds incur=20
corporate expenses - largely payments to the funds' managers - that=20
average about 100 basis points, a levy likely to cut the returns their=20
investors earn by 10% or more over time.  Charlie and I make no promises=20
about Berkshire's results.  We do promise you, however, that virtually=20
all of the gains Berkshire makes will end up with shareholders.  We are=20
here to make money with you, not off you.


<b>The Relationship of Intrinsic Value to Market Price</b>

     In last year's letter, with Berkshire shares selling at $36,000, I=20
told you:  (1) Berkshire's gain in market value in recent years had=20
outstripped its gain in intrinsic value, even though the latter gain had=20
been highly satisfactory; (2) that kind of overperformance could not=20
continue indefinitely; (3) Charlie and I did not at that moment consider=20
Berkshire to be undervalued.

     Since I set down those cautions, Berkshire's intrinsic value has=20
increased very significantly - aided in a major way by a stunning=20
performance at GEICO that I will tell you more about later - while the=20
market price of our shares has changed little.  This, of course, means=20
that in 1996 Berkshire's stock underperformed the business. =20
Consequently, today's price/value relationship is both much different=20
from what it was a year ago and, as Charlie and I see it, more=20
appropriate.

     Over time, the aggregate gains made by Berkshire shareholders must=20
of necessity match the business gains of the company.  When the stock=20
temporarily overperforms or underperforms the business, a limited number=20
of shareholders - either sellers or buyers - receive outsized benefits at=
=20
the expense of those they trade with.  Generally, the sophisticated have=20
an edge over the innocents in this game.

     Though our primary goal is to maximize the amount that our=20
shareholders, in total, reap from their ownership of Berkshire, we wish=20
also to minimize the benefits going to some shareholders at the expense=20
of others.  These are goals we would have were we managing a family=20
partnership, and we believe they make equal sense for the manager of a=20
public company.  In a partnership, fairness requires that partnership=20
interests be valued equitably when partners enter or exit; in a public=20
company, fairness prevails when market price and intrinsic value are in=20
sync.  Obviously, they won't always meet that ideal, but a manager - by=20
his policies and communications - can do much to foster equity.

     Of course, the longer a shareholder holds his shares, the more=20
bearing Berkshire's business results will have on his financial=20
experience - and the less it will matter what premium or discount to=20
intrinsic value prevails when he buys and sells his stock.  That's one=20
reason we hope to attract owners with long-term horizons.  Overall, I=20
think we have succeeded in that pursuit.  Berkshire probably ranks number=
=20
one among large American corporations in the percentage of its shares=20
held by owners with a long-term view.


<b>Acquisitions of 1996</b>

     We made two acquisitions in 1996, both possessing exactly the=20
qualities we seek - excellent business economics and an outstanding=20
manager.

     The first acquisition was Kansas Bankers Surety (KBS), an insurance=20
company whose name describes its specialty.  The company, which does=20
business in 22 states, has an extraordinary underwriting record, achieved=
=20
through the efforts of Don Towle, an extraordinary manager.  Don has=20
developed first-hand relationships with hundreds of bankers and knows=20
every detail of his operation.  He thinks of himself as running a company=
=20
that is "his," an attitude we treasure at Berkshire.  Because of its=20
relatively small size, we placed KBS with Wesco, our 80%-owned=20
subsidiary, which has wanted to expand its insurance operations.

     You might be interested in the carefully-crafted and sophisticated=20
acquisition strategy that allowed Berkshire to nab this deal.  Early in=20
1996 I was invited to the 40th birthday party of my nephew's wife, Jane=20
Rogers.  My taste for social events being low, I immediately, and in my=20
standard, gracious way, began to invent reasons for skipping the event. =20
The party planners then countered brilliantly by offering me a seat next=20
to a man I always enjoy, Jane's dad, Roy Dinsdale - so I went.

     The party took place on January 26.  Though the music was loud - Why=
=20
must bands play as if they will be paid by the decibel? - I just managed=20
to hear Roy say he'd come from a directors meeting at Kansas Bankers=20
Surety, a company I'd always admired.  I shouted back that he should let=20
me know if it ever became available for purchase.

     On February 12, I got the following letter from Roy:  "Dear Warren:=20
Enclosed is the annual financial information on Kansas Bankers Surety. =20
This is the company that we talked about at Janie's party.  If I can be=20
of any further help, please let me know."  On February 13, I told Roy we=20
would pay $75 million for the company - and before long we had a deal. =20
I'm now scheming to get invited to Jane's next party.

     Our other acquisition in 1996 - FlightSafety International, the=20
world's leader in the training of pilots - was far larger, at about $1.5=20
billion, but had an equally serendipitous origin.  The heroes of this=20
story are first, Richard Sercer, a Tucson aviation consultant, and=20
second, his wife, Alma Murphy, an ophthalmology graduate of Harvard=20
Medical School, who in 1990 wore down her husband's reluctance and got=20
him to buy Berkshire stock.  Since then, the two have attended all our=20
Annual Meetings, but I didn't get to know them personally.

     Fortunately, Richard had also been a long-time shareholder of=20
FlightSafety, and it occurred to him last year that the two companies=20
would make a good fit.  He knew our acquisition criteria, and he thought=20
that Al Ueltschi, FlightSafety's 79-year-old CEO, might want to make a=20
deal that would both give him a home for his company and a security in=20
payment that he would feel comfortable owning throughout his lifetime. =20
So in July, Richard wrote Bob Denham, CEO of Salomon Inc, suggesting that=
=20
he explore the possibility of a merger.

     Bob took it from there, and on September 18, Al and I met in New=20
York.  I had long been familiar with FlightSafety's business, and in=20
about 60 seconds I knew that Al was exactly our kind of manager.  A month=
=20
later, we had a contract.  Because Charlie and I wished to minimize the=20
issuance of Berkshire shares, the transaction we structured gave=20
FlightSafety shareholders a choice of cash or stock but carried terms=20
that encouraged those who were tax-indifferent to take cash.  This nudge=20
led to about 51% of FlightSafety's shares being exchanged for cash, 41%=20
for Berkshire A and 8% for Berkshire B.

     Al has had a lifelong love affair with aviation and actually piloted=
=20
Charles Lindbergh.  After a barnstorming career in the 1930s, he began=20
working for Juan Trippe, Pan Am's legendary chief.  In 1951, while still=20
at Pan Am, Al founded FlightSafety, subsequently building it into a=20
simulator manufacturer and a worldwide trainer of pilots (single-engine,=20
helicopter, jet and marine).  The company operates in 41 locations,=20
outfitted with 175 simulators of planes ranging from the very small, such=
=20
as Cessna 210s, to Boeing 747s.  Simulators are not cheap - they can cost=
=20
as much as $19 million  - so this business, unlike many of our=20
operations, is capital intensive.  About half of the company's revenues=20
are derived from the training of corporate pilots, with most of the=20
balance coming from airlines and the military.

     Al may be 79, but he looks and acts about 55.  He will run=20
operations just as he has in the past:  We never fool with success.  I=20
have told him that though we don't believe in splitting Berkshire stock,=20
we will split his age 2-for-1 when he hits 100.

     An observer might conclude from our hiring practices that Charlie=20
and I were traumatized early in life by an EEOC bulletin on age=20
discrimination.  The real explanation, however, is self-interest:  It's=20
difficult to teach a new dog old tricks.  The many Berkshire managers who=
=20
are past 70 hit home runs today at the same pace that long ago gave them=20
reputations as young slugging sensations.  Therefore, to get a job with=20
us, just employ the tactic of the 76-year-old who persuaded a dazzling=20
beauty of 25 to marry him.  "How did you ever get her to accept?" asked=20
his envious contemporaries.  The comeback:  "I told her I was 86."

<b>                	* * * * * * * * * * * *</b>

     And now we pause for our usual commercial:  If you own a large=20
business with good economic characteristics and wish to become associated=
=20
with an exceptional collection of businesses having similar=20
characteristics, Berkshire may well be the home you seek.  Our=20
requirements are set forth on page 21.  If your company meets them - and=20
if I fail to make the next birthday party you attend - give me a call.


<b>Insurance Operations - Overview</b>

     Our insurance business was terrific in 1996.  In both primary=20
insurance, where GEICO is our main unit, and in our "super-cat"=20
reinsurance business, results were outstanding.

     As we've explained in past reports, what counts in our insurance=20
business is, first, the amount of "float" we generate and, second, its=20
cost to us.  These are matters that are important for you to understand=20
because float is a major component of Berkshire's intrinsic value that is=
=20
not reflected in book value.

     To begin with, float is money we hold but don't own.  In an=20
insurance operation, float arises because premiums are received before=20
losses are paid.  Secondly, the premiums that an insurer takes in=20
typically do not cover the losses and expenses it eventually must pay. =20
That leaves it running an "underwriting loss," which is the cost of=20
float.  An insurance business has value if its cost of float over time is=
=20
less than the cost the company would otherwise incur to obtain funds. =20
But the business is an albatross if the cost of its float is higher than=20
market rates for money.

     As the numbers in the following table show, Berkshire's insurance=20
business has been a huge winner.  For the table, we have calculated our=20
float -  which we generate in large amounts relative to our premium=20
volume - by adding loss reserves, loss adjustment reserves, funds held=20
under reinsurance assumed and unearned premium reserves, and then=20
subtracting agents' balances, prepaid acquisition costs, prepaid taxes=20
and deferred charges applicable to assumed reinsurance.  Our cost of=20
float is determined by our underwriting loss or profit.  In those years=20
when we have had an underwriting profit, such as the last four, our cost=20
of float has been negative.  In effect, we have been paid for holding=20
money.

