     Since 1967, when we entered the insurance business, our float has=20
grown at an annual compounded rate of 22.3%.  In more years than not, our=
=20
cost of funds has been less than nothing.  This access to "free" money has=
=20
boosted Berkshire's performance in a major way.  Moreover, our acquisition=
=20
of GEICO materially increases the probability that we can continue to=20
obtain "free" funds in increasing amounts.


<b>Super-Cat Insurance</b>

     As in the past three years, we once again stress that the good results=
=20
we are reporting for Berkshire stem in part from our super-cat business=20
having a lucky year.  In this operation, we sell policies that insurance=20
and reinsurance companies buy to protect themselves from the effects of=20
mega-catastrophes.  Since truly major catastrophes are rare occurrences,=20
our super-cat business can be expected to show large profits in most years=
=20
- and to record a huge loss occasionally.  In other words, the=20
attractiveness of our super-cat business will take a great many years to=20
measure. <i> What you must understand, however, is that a truly terrible ye=
ar=20
in the super-cat business is not a possibility - it's a certainty.  The=20
only question is when it will come.</i>

     I emphasize this lugubrious point because I would not want you to=20
panic and sell your Berkshire stock upon hearing that some large=20
catastrophe had cost us a significant amount.  If you would tend to react=
=20
that way, you should not own Berkshire shares now, just as you should=20
entirely avoid owning stocks if a crashing market would lead you to panic=
=20
and sell.  Selling fine businesses on "scary" news is usually a bad=20
decision.  (Robert Woodruff, the business genius who built Coca-Cola over=
=20
many decades and who owned a huge position in the company, was once asked=
=20
when it might be a good time to sell Coke stock.  Woodruff had a simple=20
answer:  "I don't know.  I've never sold any.")

     In our super-cat operation, our customers are insurers that are=20
exposed to major earnings volatility and that wish to reduce it.  The=20
product we sell -  for what we hope is an appropriate price -  is our=20
willingness to shift that volatility to our own books.  Gyrations in=20
Berkshire's earnings don't bother us in the least:  Charlie and I would=20
much rather earn a lumpy 15% over time than a smooth 12%.  (After all, our=
=20
earnings swing wildly on a daily and weekly basis - why should we demand=20
that smoothness accompany each orbit that the earth makes of the sun?)  We=
=20
are most comfortable with that thinking, however, when we have=20
shareholder/partners who can also accept volatility, and that's why we=20
regularly repeat our cautions.

     We took on some major super-cat exposures during 1996.  At mid-year we=
=20
wrote a contract with Allstate that covers Florida hurricanes, and though=
=20
there are no definitive records that would allow us to prove this point, we=
=20
believe that to have<i> then</i> been the largest single catastrophe risk e=
ver=20
assumed by one company for its own account.  Later in the year, however, we=
=20
wrote a policy for the California Earthquake Authority that goes into=20
effect on April 1, 1997, and that exposes us to a loss more than twice that=
=20
possible under the Florida contract.  Again we retained all the risk for=20
our own account.  Large as these coverages are, Berkshire's after-tax=20
"worst-case" loss from a true mega-catastrophe is probably no more than=20
$600 million, which is less than 3% of our book value and 1.5% of our marke=
t=20
value.  To gain some perspective on this exposure, look at the table on=20
page 2 and note the much greater volatility that security markets have=20
delivered us.

     In the super-cat business, we have three major competitive advantages.=
=20
First, the parties buying reinsurance from us know that we both can and=20
will pay under the most adverse of circumstances.  Were a truly cataclysmic=
=20
disaster to occur, it is not impossible that a financial panic would=20
quickly follow.  If that happened, there could well be respected reinsurers=
=20
that would have difficulty paying at just the moment that their clients=20
faced extraordinary needs. Indeed, one reason we never "lay off" part of=20
the risks we insure is that we have reservations about our ability to=20
collect from others when disaster strikes.  When it's Berkshire promising,=
=20
insureds know with certainty that they can collect promptly.

     Our second advantage - somewhat related - is subtle but important. =20
After a mega-catastrophe, insurers might well find it difficult to obtain=
=20
reinsurance even though their need for coverage would then be particularly=
=20
great.  At such a time, Berkshire would without question have very=20
substantial capacity available - but it will naturally be our long-standing=
=20
clients that have first call on it.  That business reality has made major=
=20
insurers and reinsurers throughout the world realize the desirability of=20
doing business with us.  Indeed, we are currently getting sizable "stand-
by" fees from reinsurers that are simply nailing down their ability to get=
=20
coverage from us should the market tighten.

     Our final competitive advantage is that we can provide dollar=20
coverages of a size neither matched nor approached elsewhere in the=20
industry.  Insurers looking for huge covers know that a single call to=20
Berkshire will produce a firm and immediate offering.

     A few facts about our exposure to California earthquakes - our largest=
=20
risk - seem in order.  The Northridge quake of 1994 laid homeowners' losses=
=20
on insurers that greatly exceeded what computer models had told them to=20
expect.  Yet the intensity of that quake was mild compared to the "worst-
case" possibility for California.  Understandably, insurers became - ahem -=
=20
shaken and started contemplating a retreat from writing earthquake coverage=
=20
into their homeowners' policies.

     In a thoughtful response, Chuck Quackenbush, California's insurance=20
commissioner, designed a new residential earthquake policy to be written by=
=20
a state-sponsored insurer, The California Earthquake Authority.  This=20
entity, which went into operation on December 1, 1996, needed large layers=
=20
of reinsurance - and that's where we came in.  Berkshire's layer of=20
approximately $1 billion will be called upon if the Authority's aggregate=
=20
losses in the period ending March 31, 2001 exceed about $5 billion.  (The=
=20
press originally reported larger figures, but these would have applied only=
=20
if all California insurers had entered into the arrangement; instead only=
=20
72% signed up.)

     So what are the true odds of our having to make a payout during the=20
policy's term?  We don't know - nor do we think computer models will help=
=20
us, since we believe the precision they project is a chimera.  In fact,=20
such models can lull decision-makers into a false sense of security and=20
thereby increase their chances of making a really huge mistake.  We've=20
already seen such debacles in both insurance and investments.  Witness=20
"portfolio insurance," whose destructive effects in the 1987 market crash=
=20
led one wag to observe that it was the computers that should have been=20
jumping out of windows.

     Even if perfection in assessing risks is unattainable, insurers can=20
underwrite sensibly.  After all, you need not know a man's precise age to=
=20
know that he is old enough to vote nor know his exact weight to recognize=
=20
his need to diet.  In insurance, it is essential to remember that virtually=
=20
all surprises are unpleasant, and with that in mind we try to price our=20
super-cat exposures so that about 90% of total premiums end up being=20
eventually paid out in losses and expenses.  Over time, we will find out=20
how smart our pricing has been, but that will not be quickly.  The super-
cat business is just like the investment business in that it often takes a=
=20
long time to find out whether you knew what you were doing.

     What I can state with certainty, however, is that we have the best=20
person in the world to run our super-cat business:  Ajit Jain, whose value=
=20
to Berkshire is simply enormous.  In the reinsurance field, disastrous=20
propositions abound.  I know that because I personally embraced all too=20
many of these in the 1970s and also because GEICO has a large runoff=20
portfolio made up of foolish contracts written in the early-1980s, able=20
though its then-management was.  Ajit, I can assure you, won't make=20
mistakes of this type.

     I have mentioned that a mega-catastrophe might cause a catastrophe in=
=20
the financial markets, a possibility that is unlikely but not far-fetched.=
=20
Were the catastrophe a quake in California of sufficient magnitude to tap=
=20
our coverage, we would almost certainly be damaged in other ways as well. =
=20
For example, See's, Wells Fargo and Freddie Mac could be hit hard.  All in=
=20
all, though, we can handle this aggregation of exposures.

     In this respect, as in others, we try to "reverse engineer" our future=
=20
at Berkshire, bearing in mind Charlie's dictum:  "All I want to know is=20
where I'm going to die so I'll never go there."  (Inverting really works: =
=20
Try singing country western songs backwards and you will quickly regain=20
your house, your car and your wife.)  If we can't tolerate a possible=20
consequence, remote though it may be, we steer clear of planting its seeds.=
=20
That is why we don't borrow big amounts and why we make sure that our=20
super-cat business losses, large though the maximums may sound, will not=20
put a major dent in Berkshire's intrinsic value.=20


<b>Insurance - GEICO and Other Primary Operations</b>

     When we moved to total ownership of GEICO early last year, our=20
expectations were high - and they are all being exceeded.  That is true=20
from both a business and personal perspective:  GEICO's operating chief,=20
Tony Nicely, is a superb business manager and a delight to work with. =20
Under almost any conditions, GEICO would be an exceptionally valuable=20
asset.  With Tony at the helm, it is reaching levels of performance that=20
the organization would only a few years ago have thought impossible.

     There's nothing esoteric about GEICO's success:  The company's=20
competitive strength flows directly from its position as a low-cost=20
operator.  Low costs permit low prices, and low prices attract and retain=
=20
good policyholders.  The final segment of a virtuous circle is drawn when=
=20
policyholders recommend us to their friends.  GEICO gets more than one=20
million referrals annually and these produce more than half of our new=20
business,  an advantage that gives us enormous savings in acquisition=20
expenses - and that makes our costs still lower.

     This formula worked in spades for GEICO in 1996:  Its voluntary auto=
=20
policy count grew 10%.  During the previous 20 years, the company's best-
ever growth for a year had been 8%, a rate achieved only once.  Better yet,=
=20
the growth in voluntary policies accelerated during the year, led by major=
=20
gains in the nonstandard market, which has been an underdeveloped area at=
=20
GEICO.  I focus here on voluntary policies because the involuntary business=
=20
we get from assigned risk pools and the like is unprofitable.  Growth in=20
that sector is most unwelcome.

     GEICO's growth would mean nothing if it did not produce reasonable=20
underwriting profits.  Here, too, the news is good:  Last year we hit our=
=20
underwriting targets and then some.  Our goal, however, is not to widen our=
=20
profit margin but rather to enlarge the price advantage we offer customers.=
=20
Given that strategy, we believe that 1997's growth will easily top that of=
=20
last year.

     We expect new competitors to enter the direct-response market, and=20
some of our existing competitors are likely to expand geographically. =20
Nonetheless, the economies of scale we enjoy should allow us to maintain or=
=20
even widen the protective moat surrounding our economic castle.  We do best=
=20
on costs in geographical areas in which we enjoy high market penetration. =
=20
As our policy count grows, concurrently delivering gains in penetration, we=
=20
expect to drive costs materially lower.  GEICO's sustainable cost advantage=
=20
is what attracted me to the company way back in 1951, when the entire=20
business was valued at $7 million.  It is also why I felt Berkshire should=
=20
pay $2.3 billion last year for the 49% of the company that we didn't then=
=20
own.

     Maximizing the results of a wonderful business requires management and=
=20
focus.  Lucky for us, we have in Tony a superb manager whose business focus=
=20
never wavers.  Wanting also to get the entire GEICO organization=20
concentrating as he does, we needed a compensation plan that was itself=20
sharply focused - and immediately after our purchase, we put one in.

     Today, the bonuses received by dozens of top executives, starting with=
=20
Tony, are based upon only two key variables:  (1) growth in voluntary auto=
=20
policies and (2) underwriting profitability on "seasoned" auto business=20
(meaning policies that have been on the books for more than one year).  In=
=20
addition, we use the same yardsticks to calculate the annual contribution=
=20
to the company's profit-sharing plan. <i> Everyone</i> at GEICO knows what =
counts.

     The GEICO plan exemplifies Berkshire's incentive compensation=20
principles:  Goals should be (1) tailored to the economics of the specific=
=20
operating business; (2) simple in character so that the degree to which=20
they are being realized can be easily measured; and (3) directly related to=
=20
the daily activities of plan participants.  As a corollary, we shun=20
"lottery ticket" arrangements, such as options on Berkshire shares, whose=
=20
ultimate value - which could range from zero to huge - is totally out of=20
the control of the person whose behavior we would like to affect.  In our=
=20
view, a system that produces quixotic payoffs will not only be wasteful for=
=20
owners but may actually discourage the focused behavior we value in=20
managers.

     Every quarter, all 9,000 GEICO associates can see the results that=20
determine our profit-sharing plan contribution.  In 1996, they enjoyed the=
=20
experience because the plan literally went off the chart that had been=20
constructed at the start of the year.  Even I knew the answer to that=20
problem:  Enlarge the chart.  Ultimately, the results called for a record=
=20
contribution of 16.9% ($40 million), compared to a five-year average of=20
less than 10% for the comparable plans previously in effect.  Furthermore,=
=20
at Berkshire, we never greet good work by raising the bar.  If GEICO's=20
performance continues to improve, we will happily keep on making larger=20
charts.

     Lou Simpson continues to manage GEICO's money in an outstanding=20
manner:  Last year, the equities in his portfolio outdid the S&amp;P 500 by=
 6.2=20
percentage points.  In Lou's part of GEICO's operation, we again tie=20
compensation to performance - but to investment performance over a four-
year period, not to underwriting results nor to the performance of GEICO as=
=20
a whole.  We think it foolish for an insurance company to pay bonuses that=
=20
are tied to overall corporate results when great work on one side of the=20
business - underwriting or investment - could conceivably be completely=20
neutralized by bad work on the other.  If you bat .350 at Berkshire, you=20
can be sure you will get paid commensurately even if the rest of the team=
=20
bats .200.  In Lou and Tony, however, we are lucky to have Hall-of-Famers=
=20
in both key positions.

<b>                	* * * * * * * * * * * *</b>

     Though they are, of course, smaller than GEICO, our other primary=20
insurance operations turned in equally stunning results last year. =20
National Indemnity's traditional business had a combined ratio of 74.2 and,=
=20
as usual, developed a large amount of float compared to premium volume. =20
Over the last three years, this segment of our business, run by Don=20
Wurster, has had an average combined ratio of 83.0.  Our homestate=20
operation, managed by Rod Eldred, recorded a combined ratio of 87.1 even=20
though it absorbed the expenses of expanding to new states.  Rod's three-
year combined ratio is an amazing 83.2.  Berkshire's workers' compensation=
=20
business, run out of California by Brad Kinstler, has now moved into six=20
other states and, despite the costs of that expansion, again achieved an=20
excellent underwriting profit.  Finally, John Kizer, at Central States=20
Indemnity, set new records for premium volume while generating good=20
earnings from underwriting.  In aggregate, our smaller insurance operations=
=20
(now including Kansas Bankers Surety) have an underwriting record virtually=
=20
unmatched in the industry.  Don, Rod, Brad and John have all created=20
significant value for Berkshire, and we believe there is more to come.


<b>Taxes</b>

     In 1961, President Kennedy said that we should ask not what our=20
country can do for us, but rather ask what we can do for our country.  Last=
=20
year we decided to give his suggestion a try - and who says it never hurts=
=20
to ask?  We were told to mail $860 million in income taxes to the U.S.=20
Treasury.

     Here's a little perspective on that figure:  If an equal amount had=20
been paid by only 2,000 other taxpayers, the government would have had a=20
balanced budget in 1996 without needing a dime of taxes - income or Social=
=20
Security or what have you - from <i>any</i> other American.  Berkshire=20
shareholders can truly say, "I gave at the office."

     Charlie and I believe that large tax payments by Berkshire are=20
entirely fitting.  The contribution we thus make to society's well-being is=
=20
at most only proportional to its contribution to ours.  Berkshire prospers=
=20
in America as it would nowhere else.


