<b>"Look-Through" Earnings</b>

     Reported earnings are a poor measure of economic progress at=20
Berkshire, in part because the numbers shown in the table presented=20
earlier include only the dividends we receive from investees - though=20
these dividends typically represent only a small fraction of the earnings=
=20
attributable to our ownership.  Not that we mind this division of money,=20
since on balance we regard the undistributed earnings of investees as=20
more valuable to us than the portion paid out.  The reason is simple: =20
Our investees often have the opportunity to reinvest earnings at high=20
rates of return.  So why should we want them paid out?

     To depict something closer to economic reality at Berkshire than=20
reported earnings, though, we employ the concept of "look-through"=20
earnings.  As we calculate these, they consist of: (1) the operating=20
