     Our portfolio shows little change:  We continue to make more money=20
when snoring than when active.

     Inactivity strikes us as intelligent behavior.  Neither we nor most=20
business managers would dream of feverishly trading highly-profitable=20
subsidiaries because a small move in the Federal Reserve's discount rate=20
was predicted or because some Wall Street pundit had reversed his views=20
on the market.  Why, then, should we behave differently with our minority=
=20
positions in wonderful businesses?  The art of investing in public=20
companies successfully is little different from the art of successfully=20
acquiring subsidiaries.  In each case you simply want to acquire, at a=20
sensible price, a business with excellent economics and able, honest=20
management.  Thereafter, you need only monitor whether these qualities=20
are being preserved.

     When carried out capably, an investment strategy of that type will=20
often result in its practitioner owning a few securities that will come=20
to represent a very large portion of his portfolio.  This investor would=20
get a similar result if he followed a policy of purchasing an interest=20
in, say, 20% of the future earnings of a number of outstanding college=20
basketball stars.  A handful of these would go on to achieve NBA stardom,=
=20
and the investor's take from them would soon dominate his royalty stream.=
=20
To suggest that this investor should sell off portions of his most=20
successful investments simply because they have come to dominate his=20
portfolio is akin to suggesting that the Bulls trade Michael Jordan=20
because he has become so important to the team.

     In studying the investments we have made in both subsidiary=20
companies and common stocks, you will see that we favor businesses and=20
industries unlikely to experience major change.  The reason for that is=20
simple:  Making either type of purchase, we are searching for operations=20
that we believe are virtually certain to possess enormous competitive=20
strength ten or twenty years from now.  A fast-changing industry=20
environment may offer the chance for huge wins, but it precludes the=20
certainty we seek.

     I should emphasize that, as citizens, Charlie and I welcome change:=20
Fresh ideas, new products, innovative processes and the like cause our=20
country's standard of living to rise, and that's clearly good.  As=20
investors, however, our reaction to a fermenting industry is much like=20
our attitude toward space exploration:  We applaud the endeavor but=20
prefer to skip the ride.

     Obviously all businesses change to some extent.  Today, See's is=20
different in many ways from what it was in 1972 when we bought it:  It=20
offers a different assortment of candy, employs different machinery and=20
sells through different distribution channels.  But the reasons why=20
people today buy boxed chocolates, and why they buy them from us rather=20
than from someone else, are virtually unchanged from what they were in=20
the 1920s when the See family was building the business.  Moreover, these=
=20
motivations are not likely to change over the next 20 years, or even 50.

     We look for similar predictability in marketable securities.  Take=20
Coca-Cola:  The zeal and imagination with which Coke products are sold=20
has burgeoned under Roberto Goizueta, who has done an absolutely=20
incredible job in creating value for his shareholders.  Aided by Don=20
Keough and Doug Ivester, Roberto has rethought and improved every aspect=20
of the company.  But the fundamentals of the business - the qualities=20
that underlie Coke's competitive dominance and stunning economics - have=20
remained constant through the years.

     I was recently studying the 1896 report of Coke (and you think that=20
<i>you</i> are behind in your reading!).  At that time Coke, though it was=
=20
already the leading soft drink, had been around for only a decade.  But=20
its blueprint for the next 100 years was already drawn.  Reporting sales=20
of $148,000 that year, Asa Candler, the company's president, said:  "We=20
have not lagged in our efforts to go into all the world teaching that=20
Coca-Cola is the article, par excellence, for the health and good feeling=
=20
of all people."  Though "health" may have been a reach, I love the fact=20
that Coke still relies on Candler's basic theme today - a century later.=20
Candler went on to say, just as Roberto could now, "No article of like=20
character has ever so firmly entrenched itself in public favor."  Sales=20
of syrup that year, incidentally, were 116,492 gallons versus about 3.2=20
billion in 1996.

     I can't resist one more Candler quote:  "Beginning this year about=20
March 1st . . . we employed ten traveling salesmen by means of which,=20
with systematic correspondence from the office, we covered almost the=20
territory of the Union."  That's my kind of sales force.

     Companies such as Coca-Cola and Gillette might well be labeled "The=20
Inevitables."  Forecasters may differ a bit in their predictions of=20
exactly how much soft drink or shaving-equipment business these companies=
=20
will be doing in ten or twenty years.  Nor is our talk of inevitability=20
meant to play down the vital work that these companies must continue to=20
carry out, in such areas as manufacturing, distribution, packaging and=20
product innovation.  In the end, however, no sensible observer - not even=
=20
these companies' most vigorous competitors, assuming they are assessing=20
the matter honestly - questions that Coke and Gillette will dominate=20
their fields worldwide for an investment lifetime.  Indeed, their=20
dominance will probably strengthen.  Both companies have significantly=20
expanded their already huge shares of market during the past ten years,=20
and all signs point to their repeating that performance in the next=20
decade.

     Obviously many companies in high-tech businesses or embryonic=20
industries will grow much faster in percentage terms than will The=20
Inevitables.  But I would rather be certain of a good result than hopeful=
=20
of a great one.

     Of course, Charlie and I can identify only a few Inevitables, even=20
after a lifetime of looking for them.  Leadership alone provides no=20
certainties:  Witness the shocks some years back at General Motors, IBM=20
and Sears, all of which had enjoyed long periods of seeming=20
invincibility.  Though some industries or lines of business exhibit=20
characteristics that endow leaders with virtually insurmountable=20
advantages, and that tend to establish Survival of the Fattest as almost=20
a natural law, most do not.  Thus, for every Inevitable, there are dozens=
=20
of Impostors, companies now riding high but vulnerable to competitive=20
attacks.  Considering what it takes to be an Inevitable, Charlie and I=20
recognize that we will never be able to come up with a Nifty Fifty or=20
even a Twinkling Twenty.  To the Inevitables in our portfolio, therefore,=
=20
we add a few "Highly Probables."

     You can, of course, pay too much for even the best of businesses. =20
The overpayment risk surfaces periodically and, in our opinion, may now=20
be quite high for the purchasers of virtually all stocks, The Inevitables=
=20
included.  Investors making purchases in an overheated market need to=20
recognize that it may often take an extended period for the value of even=
=20
an outstanding company to catch up with the price they paid.

     A far more serious problem occurs when the management of a great=20
company gets sidetracked and neglects its wonderful base business while=20
purchasing other businesses that are so-so or worse.  When that happens,=20
the suffering of investors is often prolonged.  Unfortunately, that is=20
precisely what transpired years ago at both Coke and Gillette.  (Would=20
you believe that a few decades back they were growing shrimp at Coke and=20
exploring for oil at Gillette?)  Loss of focus is what most worries=20
Charlie and me when we contemplate investing in businesses that in=20
general look outstanding.  All too often, we've seen value stagnate in=20
the presence of hubris or of boredom that caused the attention of=20
managers to wander.  That's not going to happen again at Coke and=20
Gillette, however - not given their current and prospective managements.
<b>
                     * * * * * * * * * * * *
</b>
     Let me add a few thoughts about your own investments.  Most=20
investors, both institutional and individual, will find that the best way=
=20
to own common stocks is through an index fund that charges minimal fees.=20
Those following this path are sure to beat the net results (after fees=20
and expenses) delivered by the great majority of investment=20
professionals.

     Should you choose, however, to construct your own portfolio, there=20
are a few thoughts worth remembering.  Intelligent investing is not=20
complex, though that is far from saying that it is easy.  What an=20
investor needs is the ability to correctly evaluate selected businesses.=20
Note that word "selected":  You don't have to be an expert on every=20
company, or even many.  You only have to be able to evaluate companies=20
within your circle of competence.  The size of that circle is not very=20
important; knowing its boundaries, however, is vital.

     To invest successfully, you need not understand beta, efficient=20
markets, modern portfolio theory, option pricing or emerging markets. =20
You may, in fact, be better off knowing nothing of these.  That, of=20
course, is not the prevailing view at most business schools, whose=20
finance curriculum tends to be dominated by such subjects.  In our view,=20
though, investment students need only two well-taught courses - How to=20
Value a Business, and How to Think About Market Prices.

     Your goal as an investor should simply be to purchase, at a rational=
=20
price, a part interest in an easily-understandable business whose=20
earnings are virtually certain to be materially higher five, ten and=20
twenty years from now.  Over time, you will find only a few companies=20
that meet these standards - so when you see one that qualifies, you=20
should buy a meaningful amount of stock.  You must also resist the=20
temptation to stray from your guidelines:  If you aren't willing to own a=
=20
stock for ten years, don't even think about owning it for ten minutes. =20
Put together a portfolio of companies whose aggregate earnings march=20
upward over the years, and so also will the portfolio's market value.

     Though it's seldom recognized, this is the exact approach that has=20
produced gains for Berkshire shareholders:  Our look-through earnings=20
have grown at a good clip over the years, and our stock price has risen=20
correspondingly.  Had those gains in earnings not materialized, there=20
would have been little increase in Berkshire's value.

     The greatly enlarged earnings base we now enjoy will inevitably=20
cause our future gains to lag those of the past.  We will continue,=20
however, to push in the directions we always have.  We will try to build=20
earnings by running our present businesses well - a job made easy because=
=20
of the extraordinary talents of our operating managers - and by=20
purchasing other businesses, in whole or in part, that are not likely to=20
be roiled by change and that possess important competitive advantages.


<b>USAir</b>

     When Richard Branson, the wealthy owner of Virgin Atlantic Airways,=20
was asked how to become a millionaire, he had a quick answer:  "There's=20
really nothing to it.  Start as a billionaire and then buy an airline." =20
Unwilling to accept Branson's proposition on faith, your Chairman decided=
=20
in 1989 to test it by investing $358 million in a 9.25% preferred stock of=
=20
USAir.

     I liked and admired Ed Colodny, the company's then-CEO, and I still=20
do.  But my analysis of USAir's business was both superficial and wrong.=20
I was so beguiled by the company's long history of profitable=20
operations, and by the protection that ownership of a senior security=20
seemingly offered me, that I overlooked the crucial point:  USAir's=20
revenues would increasingly feel the effects of an unregulated, fiercely-
competitive market whereas its cost structure was a holdover from the=20
days when regulation protected profits.  These costs, if left unchecked,=20
portended disaster, however reassuring the airline's past record might=20
be.  (If history supplied all of the answers, the Forbes 400 would=20
consist of librarians.)

     To rationalize its costs, however, USAir needed major improvements=20
in its labor contracts - and that's something most airlines have found it=
=20
extraordinarily difficult to get, short of credibly threatening, or=20
actually entering, bankruptcy.  USAir was to be no exception. =20
Immediately after we purchased our preferred stock, the imbalance between=
=20
the company's costs and revenues began to grow explosively.  In the 1990-
1994 period, USAir lost an aggregate of $2.4 billion, a performance that=20
totally wiped out the book equity of its common stock.

     For much of this period, the company paid us our preferred=20
dividends, but in 1994 payment was suspended.  A bit later, with the=20
situation looking particularly gloomy, we wrote down our investment by=20
75%, to $89.5 million.  Thereafter, during much of 1995, I offered to=20
sell our shares at 50% of face value.  Fortunately, I was unsuccessful.

     Mixed in with my many mistakes at USAir was one thing I got right: =20
Making our investment, we wrote into the preferred contract a somewhat=20
unusual provision stipulating that "penalty dividends" - to run five=20
percentage points over the prime rate - would be accrued on any=20
arrearages.  This meant that when our 9.25% dividend was omitted for two=20
years, the unpaid amounts compounded at rates ranging between 13.25% and=20
14%.

     Facing this penalty provision, USAir had every incentive to pay=20
arrearages just as promptly as it could.  And in the second half of 1996,=
=20
when USAir turned profitable, it indeed began to pay, giving us $47.9=20
million.  We owe Stephen Wolf, the company's CEO, a huge thank-you for=20
extracting a performance from the airline that permitted this payment. =20
Even so, USAir's performance has recently been helped significantly by an=
=20
industry tailwind that may be cyclical in nature.  The company still has=20
basic cost problems that must be solved.

     In any event, the prices of USAir's publicly-traded securities tell=20
us that our preferred stock is now probably worth its par value of $358=20
million, give or take a little.  In addition, we have over the years=20
collected an aggregate of $240.5 million in dividends (including $30=20
million received in 1997).

     Early in 1996, before any accrued dividends had been paid, I tried=20
once more to unload our holdings - this time for about $335 million. =20
You're lucky:  I again failed in my attempt to snatch defeat from the=20
jaws of victory.

     In another context, a friend once asked me:  "If you're so rich, why=
=20
aren't you smart?"  After reviewing my sorry performance with USAir, you=20
may conclude he had a point.


<b>Financings</b>

     We wrote four checks to Salomon Brothers last year and in each case=20
were delighted with the work for which we were paying.  I've already=20
described one transaction: the FlightSafety purchase in which Salomon was=
=20
the initiating investment banker.  In a second deal, the firm placed a=20
small debt offering for our finance subsidiary.

     Additionally, we made two good-sized offerings through Salomon, both=
=20
with interesting aspects.  The first was our sale in May of 517,500=20
shares of Class B Common, which generated net proceeds of $565 million. =20
As I have told you before, we made this sale in response to the=20
threatened creation of unit trusts that would have marketed themselves as=
=20
Berkshire look-alikes.  In the process, they would have used our past,=20
and definitely nonrepeatable, record to entice naive small investors and=20
would have charged these innocents high fees and commissions.

     I think it would have been quite easy for such trusts to have sold=20
many billions of dollars worth of units, and I also believe that early=20
marketing successes by these trusts would have led to the formation of=20
others.  (In the securities business, whatever can be sold will be sold.)=
=20
The trusts would have meanwhile indiscriminately poured the proceeds of=20
their offerings into a supply of Berkshire shares that is fixed and=20
limited.  The likely result: a speculative bubble in our stock.  For at=20
least a time, the price jump would have been self-validating, in that it=20
would have pulled new waves of naive and impressionable investors into=20
the trusts and set off still more buying of Berkshire shares.

     Some Berkshire shareholders choosing to exit might have found that=20
outcome ideal, since they could have profited at the expense of the=20
buyers entering with false hopes.  Continuing shareholders, however,=20
would have suffered once reality set in, for at that point Berkshire=20
would have been burdened with both hundreds of thousands of unhappy,=20
indirect owners (trustholders, that is) and a stained reputation.

     Our issuance of the B shares not only arrested the sale of the=20
trusts, but provided a low-cost way for people to invest in Berkshire if=20
they still wished to after hearing the warnings we issued.  To blunt the=20
enthusiasm that brokers normally have for pushing new issues - because=20
that's where the money is - we arranged for our offering to carry a=20
commission of only 1.5%, the lowest payoff that we have ever seen in a=20
common stock underwriting.  Additionally, we made the amount of the=20
offering open-ended, thereby repelling the typical IPO buyer who looks=20
for a short-term price spurt arising from a combination of hype and=20
scarcity.

     Overall, we tried to make sure that the B stock would be purchased=20
only by investors with a long-term perspective.  Those efforts were=20
generally successful:  Trading volume in the B shares immediately=20
following the offering - a rough index of "flipping" - was far below the=20
norm for a new issue.  In the end we added about 40,000 shareholders,=20
most of whom we believe both understand what they own and share our time=20
horizons.

     Salomon could not have performed better in the handling of this=20
unusual transaction.  Its investment bankers understood perfectly what we=
=20
were trying to achieve and tailored every aspect of the offering to meet=20
these objectives.  The firm would have made far more money - perhaps ten=20
times as much - if our offering had been standard in its make-up.  But=20
the investment bankers involved made no attempt to tweak the specifics in=
=20
that direction.  Instead they came up with ideas that were counter to=20
Salomon's financial interest but that made it much more certain=20
Berkshire's goals would be reached.  Terry Fitzgerald captained this=20
effort, and we thank him for the job that he did.

     Given that background, it won't surprise you to learn that we again=20
went to Terry when we decided late in the year to sell an issue of=20
Berkshire notes that can be exchanged for a portion of the Salomon shares=
=20
that we hold.  In this instance, once again, Salomon did an absolutely=20
first-class job, selling $500 million principal amount of five-year notes=
=20
for $447.1 million.  Each $1,000 note is exchangeable into 17.65 shares=20
and is callable in three years at accreted value.  Counting the original=20
issue discount and a 1% coupon, the securities will provide a yield of 3%=
=20
to maturity for holders who do not exchange them for Salomon stock.  But=20
it seems quite likely that the notes will be exchanged before their=20
maturity.  If that happens, our interest cost will be about 1.1% for the=20
period prior to exchange.

     In recent years, it has been written that Charlie and I are unhappy=20
about all investment-banking fees.  That's dead wrong.  We have paid a=20
great many fees over the last 30 years - beginning with the check we=20
wrote to Charlie Heider upon our purchase of National Indemnity in 1967 -=
=20
and we are delighted to make payments that are commensurate with=20
performance.  In the case of the 1996 transactions at Salomon Brothers,=20
we more than got our money's worth.


<b>Miscellaneous</b>

     Though it was a close decision, Charlie and I have decided to enter=20
the 20th Century.  Accordingly, we are going to put future quarterly and=20
annual reports of Berkshire on the Internet, where they can be accessed=20
via http://www.berkshirehathaway.com.  We will always "post" these=20
reports on a Saturday so that anyone interested will have ample time to=20
digest the information before trading begins.  Our publishing schedule=20
for the next 12 months is May 17, 1997, August 16, 1997, November 15,=20
1997, and March 14, 1998.  We will also post any press releases that we=20
issue.

     At some point, we may stop mailing our quarterly reports and simply=20
post these on the Internet.  This move would eliminate significant costs.=
=20
Also, we have a large number of "street name" holders and have found=20
that the distribution of our quarterlies to them is highly erratic:  Some=
=20
holders receive their mailings weeks later than others.

     The drawback to Internet-only distribution is that many of our=20
shareholders lack computers.  Most of these holders, however, could=20
easily obtain printouts at work or through friends.  Please let me know=20
if you prefer that we continue mailing quarterlies.  We want your input -=
=20
starting with whether you even read these reports - and at a minimum will=
=20
make no change in 1997.  Also, we will definitely keep delivering the=20
annual report in its present form in addition to publishing it on the=20
Internet.

